# [[.Module]]

This project contains an empty `site` app and GoDj's identity app. The declaration
runner, generated model bundle, database configuration and HTTP application are
ready to extend. `startproject` has generated and compiled the project; database
migrations and the initial account are explicit operations.

## Run locally

Use the `godj` executable that created this project, with Go 1.26.5 in PATH.
Run these commands from this directory:

```sh
godj generate --check
godj check
godj migrations check
godj migrate
godj createsuperuser
godj runserver
```

`createsuperuser` prompts for the first administrator's username and password on
the terminal. Open `http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/` and sign in. The application also
provides the identity JSON API at `/api/identity/`; unsafe requests require the
authenticated session and its masked CSRF token. The OpenAPI document at
`/api/identity/openapi.json` requires identity model permissions.

The default database is `site.sqlite3`. Apply migrations before serving. Without
an initial account, the migrated application serves only its public home page.
After provisioning, restarting loads the stored identity and permissions without
requiring the password again.

The supplied server accepts literal loopback addresses only. Its session cookies
allow local HTTP. Review the deployment, TLS and cookie policy before creating a
separate production entrypoint.

## Change the application

- Select app definitions in `modeldef/apps.go`. Define models there or import
  the app's declaration package; neither imports generated model code.
- `modeldef/project.go` binds each app to `apps/<label>/models` and creates the
  generated project binding in `project/`.
- Edit `internal/config/config.go` for database and identity policy. It reads an
  explicit environment snapshot and opens no resources.
- Add Admin resources and authenticated API routes in `internal/site/models.go`.
  Choose exposed fields and permissions explicitly; adding a model does not
  automatically publish its fields as HTTP input.
- Keep generated Go source, `go.mod`, `go.sum`, migrations and
  `.godj/generated-manifest.json` with the project source.

After changing a model, run `godj generate`, `godj makemigrations`, inspect the
new migration, then run `godj migrate`. Stop and restart `godj runserver` to load
the changes. Generation and server startup do not apply migrations or change
stored account permissions automatically.

## Add an app

```sh
godj startapp line_items apps/line_items
```

In `modeldef/apps.go`, import the declaration package:

```go
lineitemsdef "[[.Module]]/apps/line_items/modeldef"
```

Add `lineitemsdef.Definition()` to the returned `[]schema.Definition`, preserving
the existing definitions. Then run `godj generate`. The empty app has a stable
identity and no tables. Add its models in `apps/line_items/modeldef/schema.go`,
generate again, and create/review/apply migrations as described above.

App creation and installation are separate: `startapp` writes a new directory
and reports `installed: false`. The project's explicit declaration selects the
installed apps. It does not rewrite the existing declaration, generated bundle,
database or HTTP permissions. App labels use the canonical lowercase schema
namespace; the starter derives distinct Go aliases for underscores and keywords.

To select another SQLite database, set `GODJ_SQLITE_DATABASE`. To select
PostgreSQL, unset that variable and set `GODJ_POSTGRES_URL` and
`GODJ_POSTGRES_SCHEMA` to a database/schema you own. Use the same configuration
for migration, account provisioning and the server. Do not commit credentials.

[[if .FrameworkSource]]This project uses an explicitly selected local framework checkout through the
`replace` entry in `go.mod`. Moving or sharing the project requires choosing an
available checkout or an explicit published module version.
[[else]]The framework dependency is pinned to `[[.FrameworkVersion]]` in `go.mod`.
[[end]]
## Management commands

```sh
godj help
godj commands
godj help count-groups
godj command count-groups
```

Register commands in `internal/commands/commands.go`. The separate `cmd/commands`
entrypoint can import generated models; keep the declaration runner independent
of those imports. Define typed arguments/options, then use `command.Using` to
open the same configured database for one command and close it afterward.
The global command compares linked framework contracts and validates generated
source before entering a command. It preserves the command's stdout/stderr and
exit code and never automatically retries a failed action.

`commands` and custom `help` open no database. `check` validates declaration,
generated source, command registration and the project's pure configuration
check. Commands execute with the operator's OS/database permissions; they do not
implicitly impersonate an HTTP user. A command error, output error or cleanup
failure does not prove that prior database writes rolled back.

## Creation failures

`startproject` publishes only to a new directory whose parent already exists.
It rejects existing files, directories and symbolic links. A failed candidate
build or generation leaves the destination absent and removes its owned staging
directory. Dependency resolution may need network access to the configured Go
module proxy.

A `published_...` error means the directory was already created; inspect it
before retrying. A durability or cleanup error does not mean creation was rolled
back. Errors requiring staging recovery leave the owned work for inspection.
